                <pb facs="0206" n="198" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg198"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Pacific War/8: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Batavia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Foote</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-10-30T16:00:00-05:00">October 30, 1940—4
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your October 24, 2 p.m. The Department has noted your reference to a
                    communication from the Japanese delegation (presumably to the Netherlands
                    delegation) in which it is asserted that the offers of the oil companies are
                    widely divergent both in quantity and quality from Japan’s needs and in which it
                    is requested that the Netherlands delegation comply with the Japanese proposals.
                    Is this communication in any way inconsistent with Mukai’s acceptance on October
                    19, reported in your October 21, 3 p.m.,<anchor n="40" xml:id="d240-f1"/> of the
                    quantities which the oil companies offered? Please clarify.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
